        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Pick a Partner Poker',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Pick a Partner is a stud poker variant for an even number of players. Everyone antes and receives five cards face down. All players simultaneously expose one card (roll). The highest card holder picks a partner; then the highest unpartnered player picks, until all are paired. Each partnership combines their eight remaining cards and selects three to use with their two upcards to form a five-card hand. Three betting rounds follow, with one of the three chosen cards turned face up after each round. Partners bet as individuals; the team with the best hand shares the pot, or one partner wins the whole pot if the other folded.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Pick a Partner is a stud poker variant from the Roll Your Own family. It combines ante, a simultaneous card expose (roll) for partner selection, and partnership hand formation with multiple betting rounds and gradual card reveals.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Even number of players (2, 4, or 6).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Poker chips, standard deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player antes. Dealer deals five cards face down to each player. All players simultaneously expose one card (roll). Highest card holder selects a partner; then the highest unpartnered player selects a partner, and so on until all are paired. Each partnership combines their eight remaining cards and selects three to use with their two upcards to form a five-card hand. Three betting rounds follow, with one of the three chosen cards turned face up after each round.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
